For forty-five years an old woman in a rotting officer's coat walked the streets of eighteenth-century Petersburg and answered only to the name of her dead husband, Andrei Fedorovich. At his funeral, the story goes, she told the mourners that Xenia had died. She gave away her house, took nothing but old copper kopecks that she gave away at once, and prayed all night in the open fields, where the police once followed her. Her relatives tried to have her placed under guardianship, but the officers found her sane. Why would a young widow vanish into a dead man's name? And why does Petersburg still bring her its hopes for homes, work, husbands, and children?
